# What is not part of this repository.
#
# Everything a reader needs to build or run the tree is checked in, so a rule here is a
# rule about something generated: build output, tooling state, or the folders this
# framework writes while an application runs.

## Build output

[Bb]in/
[Oo]bj/
[Dd]ebug/
[Rr]elease/
x64/
x86/
artifacts/
out/
*.binlog
[Ll]og/
[Ll]ogs/
*.log

## Test and coverage output

[Tt]est[Rr]esult*/
coverage*.json
coverage*.xml
coverage*.info
*.coverage
*.coveragexml

## NuGet

*.nupkg
*.snupkg
*.nuget.props
*.nuget.targets
project.lock.json
project.fragment.lock.json

# The packages folder, kept for a project that restores into one; its build/ folder is
# an MSBuild target and stays tracked.
**/[Pp]ackages/*
!**/[Pp]ackages/build/

## Editors and user settings

.vs/
.vscode/*
!.vscode/settings.json
!.vscode/tasks.json
!.vscode/launch.json
!.vscode/extensions.json
*.code-workspace
.idea/
*.user
*.suo
*.userosscache
*.sln.docstates
*.DotSettings.user
_ReSharper*/
*.sln.iml
.history/
.localhistory/
.vshistory/
.trae/

## Node

node_modules/
.ntvs_analysis.dat

# The vendored SSR runtime ships its dependencies. The tree under src/upstream/ssr is the
# Python version's compiled server, checked in byte for byte, and Node resolves these packages
# from the directory it is started in - there is no install step at run time or in CI. The
# blanket rule above would drop them, and the runtime would die on its first import.
!src/upstream/ssr/node/build/node_modules/
!src/upstream/ssr/node/build/node_modules/**

## Secrets and machine-specific publish settings

*.pfx
*.pubxml
*.publishsettings
*.azurePubxml

## Operating system

.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

## This framework's own output

# The cache folder an application gets beside itself: the media helpers write the sample
# files there, and run history is kept there when an application turns it on.
**/.gradio/**

# Scratch space for whoever is working in the tree.
temp/
plan/

# Tool state that belongs to a working copy rather than to the framework: session notes and
# memory written by an assistant working in the tree. Never published.
.workbuddy-ai/
